+// MapGenSymmetry.cpp - [移植侧] the symmetry passes.
+//
+// This is OUR code, not the reference implementation's: the vanilla random map
+// generator has no symmetry option at all, so there is no disassembly address to
+// cite.  What it does instead is documented where it is derived - engine/src/
+// mirror.h for the cell mapping, and the notes below for the rest.
+//
+// ===========================================================================
+// TWO PHASES, AND WHY IT HAS TO BE TWO
+//
+// A symmetric map has to be symmetric in its SHAPE, and then have its art chosen
+// for that shape.  That is what the official mirror maps do, and it is measured:
+// the official YR map MULTI_B5AD7187 is 0.996 symmetric at the shape level
+// (terrain class + elevation) and renders with 0.13% pure black.
+//
+// The first implementation here did the opposite: it generated the whole map and
+// then moved the finished cells.  That was 1.000 symmetric in the cell data and
+// rendered 2.30% black - speckles everywhere and a staircase coastline.  Moving
+// finished art cannot work, because RA2 tile art interlocks through overhangs that
+// are not mirror-symmetric, and the tile set contains no mirrored art at all
+// (tools/verify_symmetry.py measures that over all five multi-cell families).
+//
+// So:
+//
+//   MirrorShape()  right after GenerateMapBody() and before any art is stamped.
+//                  Reflects the SHAPE - the land/water mark, the height field and
+//                  the elevation - and then the engine's own stages
+//                  (GenerateTerrain, DecorateWaterTiles, CreateHills, CreateLATs)
+//                  choose every tile for the mirrored shape.  The art therefore
+//                  comes out with the orientation and the interlocking the
+//                  generator itself would have given that terrain.
+//
+//   MirrorMap()    at the end, between PruneTerrainTrees() and Cleanup().
+//                  Reflects what is PLACED rather than shaped: the overlay layer
+//                  and the four ledgers.  It deliberately does NOT touch
+//                  IsoTileTypeIndex / Height / Level any more - doing that is the
+//                  defect described above.
+//
+// Both consume no RNG and both return immediately for MapSymmetry::None, so
+// "same parameters + same seed -> byte-identical" still holds and every existing
+// map is unchanged when symmetry is off (tools/verify_symmetry.py compares
+// --symmetry 0 against the oracle map byte for byte).
+// ===========================================================================
+
